{"name":"Rococo Gold","hex":"#977447","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rococo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A style of baroque architecture and decorative art, from 18th-century France, having elaborate ornamentation.","A piece of ornamentation in this style."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the rococo style.","Over-elaborate or complicated; opulent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rococo"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rococo","blocks":[{"text":"Rococo, less commonly Roccoco, is a Western style of architecture, art, and decoration that emerged in France in the 1730s as a reaction against the Louis XIV style. It is characterized by extensive ornamentation, fluid curves, asymmetry, and a smaller scale designed to foster intimacy. It employs rocaille (shell- and pebble-like motifs) and often blends white and pastel colours with gilding and sculpted moulding, using trompe-l'œil frescoes to create spatial illusion. In the visual arts, prevalent themes are aristocratic leisure and the pastoral, depicted in stage-like settings. It is often regarded as the final expression of the Baroque movement and is sometimes called Late Baroque."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Salon_de_la_princesse_hotel_de_soubise.jpg/330px-Salon_de_la_princesse_hotel_de_soubise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rococo","wikidata":"Q122960"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg/500px-Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg/1280px-Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg/500px-Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/The_Music_Lesson_MET_DP-14272-001.jpg/500px-The_Music_Lesson_MET_DP-14272-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/The_Music_Lesson_MET_DP-14272-001.jpg/1280px-The_Music_Lesson_MET_DP-14272-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_camp